# Project Defaults

[P2] Repository evidence overrides these. Do not migrate stack, database, UI framework, package manager, or tooling unless the user asks.

## Proportional Engineering

Match engineering depth to the requested maturity; never silently promote a prototype or MVP into a production program.

- Prototype: disposable, inspectable code that resolves the named uncertainty; claim nothing about completeness, durability, or readiness.
- MVP: the smallest real end-to-end user outcome with basic error handling and data integrity; defer scale, extension points, and secondary workflows.
- Core: correct, cohesive, testable central domain behavior; peripheral integrations and polish stay outside unless required.
- Usable: primary journeys, recovery paths, accessibility, documentation, and operational ergonomics for regular use.
- Production: reliability, security, observability, recovery, performance, and deployment controls justified by the real threat model, load, data, and SLOs.
- Business or regulated: tenancy, audit, compliance, support, and governance only when the business or regulatory contract requires them.

## Simplicity

- Prefer one implementation, owner, data path, and source of truth; reuse a proven local or standard capability before building another.
- Add an abstraction, service, compatibility layer, dependency, or configuration axis only for a current requirement. Do not build for hypothetical scale, providers, callers, attacks, or reuse; record the limit and evolve when evidence crosses it.
- Between designs that meet the goal, choose the one with fewer concepts, states, dependencies, and failure modes.
- State the basis of every cap, gate, guard, or refusal where it is defined: an observed failure, a measured external limit, or a stated requirement. "Engineering bound", "policy choice", or "not yet observed" is not a basis; leave the path open and note the limit.
- Enforce each safety property at one boundary; add a second layer only after naming an input the first passes and the second stops.
- Do not accept, parse, emit, model, or allowlist what nothing produces or consumes yet: reserved values, input accepted only to be refused, fields no code sets, and events nothing reads arrive with their feature.
- When a new path replaces an old one, delete the old path's code, tests, docs, and schema in that change or the next, unless a named current consumer remains.
- Integrate repositories, tools, or services the user supplies as building blocks before building equivalents, and record why when you do not.
- Replace or delete a flawed design rather than stacking guards, exceptions, and compatibility branches; repeated same-class fixes mean simplify.
- Use a maintained parser, library, or protocol implementation for non-trivial domain rules when it reduces total owned complexity.
- Decline work whose complexity cost exceeds its demonstrated value; nits, speculative edge cases, and unrelated debt are not work without explicit value or user direction.
- Test user-observable behavior and stable contracts, not prose wording, documentation counts, implementation shape, or dependency internals, unless those exact bytes are a consumed interface.

## Reliability, Security, Data

- Handle, propagate, or convert errors explicitly; never silently discard a fallible result; libraries return errors instead of exiting; avoid crash-only handling in production paths unless the invariant is proven and local convention allows it.
- Validate external input at trust boundaries, use parameterized queries, and take secrets from platform stores or the environment, never from source.
- For new persistent datastores prefer PostgreSQL; keep existing choices.

## Stack

- For new UI in this project family prefer Rust Leptos or Python Reflex; keep an existing web stack (React, Vue, Svelte, raw web assets, Node services).
- Node is fine for tooling and for repos that already ship Node.

## Scripts and Maintainability

- Keep durable automation in repo scripts; keep one-off investigation scripts out of the repo unless the user asks to preserve them as tooling, runbooks, tests, or release automation.
- Name meaningful magic values; comments explain why, not what; match the file's comment density, naming, formatting, and idiom; avoid broad refactors unless the request or a safe fix needs them.
- Living docs state the current contract and open decisions once and link instead of restating. After a change is accepted, remove its handoff, evidence, and review history from specs and plans, and do not document designs nobody is building.
